render it more ductile.—{{smaller|'''Tilted steel.''' Same as ''shear-steel.''—'''To tilt up,''' in ''geol.'', to turn up or cause to incline, and, as this word is more generally used, at a somewhat steep angle.}}

'''tilt'''<sup>1</sup> (tilt), ''n.'' [< ''tilt''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'' Cf. E. dial. ''tolt'', a blow against a beam or the like.] 1. A sloping position; inclination forward, backward, or to one side: as, the ''tilt'' of a cask; to give a thing a ''tilt''.

{{smaller block|A gentleman of large proportions, . . . wearing his broad-brimmed, steeple-crowned felt hat with the least possible ''tilt'' on one side.
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Old Vol. of Life, p. 62.}}}}

2. A thrust. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Two or three of his liege subjects, whom he very dexterously put to death with the ''tilt'' of his lance.
{{right|''Addison'', Freeholder, No. 10.}}}}

3. An exercise consisting in charging with the spear, sharp or blunted, whether against an antagonist or against a mark, such as the quintain. {{smaller|During the middle ages citizens tilted on horseback, and also in boats, which were moved rapidly against one another, so that the defeated tilter was thrown into the water.}}

{{smaller block|There shalbe entertained into the said Achademy one good horsman, to teache noble men and gentlemen . . . to runne at Ringe, ''Tilte'', Towrney, and cowrse of the fielde.
{{right|''Booke of Precedence'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 4.}}}}

{{smaller block|See at the Southern Isles the tides at ''tilt'' to run.
{{right|''Drayton'', Polyolbion, ii. 219.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ''tilt'' was now opened, and certain masqued knights appeared in the course.
{{right|''I. D'Israeli'', Calam. of Authors, II. 224.}}}}
